    "created_at": "2016-07-27T14:30:01Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-07-27T14:30:01Z",
    "body": "I don't think the description in this section is clear enough. \n\n> However, the use of these characters is restricted so that any embedding or overrides generated by these characters do not start and end with different parent elements, and so that all such embeddings and overrides are explicitly terminated by a U+202C POP DIRECTIONAL FORMATTING character.\n1. this should say \"explicitly terminated by a U+202C POP DIRECTIONAL FORMATTING or U+2069   POP DIRECTIONAL ISOLATE character, according to the type of embedding.\"\n2. the algorithm also prevents, if i understood it correctly something like \n   `<p>[RLO] foo  <span> bar </span> baz [PDF] </p>`\n   since the output for the p element would be\n   1. [RLO] foo\n   2. baz [PDF]\n\nneither of these strings matches the bidirectional-algorithm formatting character range defintion, even though the embedding generated by the rlo..pdf characters do start and end with the same parent element.\n\nMaybe i'm misunderstanding something, but i think that \n`<p>foo [RLO] bar <bdi> baz [PDF] </bdi></p>`\ndoes produce the expected invalid result (because the output for the p element is 'foo [RLO] bar' only. \n\nHowever, @zcorpan seems to be making the point that there's another error that could be pointed out to users if isolated and non-isolated embeddings are mixed with overlapping ranges.  I agree with that.  If you were to convert the above markup to plain text, you'd end up with undesirable overlapping ranges as shown here:\n\n`<p>foo [RLO] bar [RLI] baz [PDF] [PDI]</p>`\n\nNote, btw, that this effect can be produced not only by certain elements that are inherently isolating, but also by adding a `dir` attribute to any element.\n\n@zcorpan am i barking up the right tree here?\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
